.he "wslib (1)                    8/11/26                    wslib (1)"
.fo "				    -#-"
.in 5
.rm 65
.bd
PROGRAM
.in +5
.br
.bd
wslib
- object file librarian


.in -5
.bd
USAGE
.in +5
.br
.bd
wslib -[flags] archive [file ...]


.in -5
.bd
FUNCTION
.in +5
.br
.bd
Wslib
collects object files into an archive that
.bd
ld
can search.  An archive is not simply a concatenation: it carries a
directory of the symbols its members define, so the linker can decide
which members it needs without reading all of them.

The point of a library is that only what is referenced is taken from
it.  A program that calls one routine from an archive of two hundred
gets one routine.  Nothing is gained by splitting a program's own
objects into a library, and something is lost - the order the linker
sees them in stops being the order they were given.

The first argument after the flags names the archive.  The rest name
members to operate on; where none are given, the operation applies to
all of them.

Exactly one of these says what to do:

.in +10
.ta 10
.ti -10
-r	Replace the named members, adding any that are not already
there.  This is the usual way to build an archive.  With -c the
archive is created if it does not exist.

.ti -10
-a	Append the named files to the end of the archive, without
looking to see whether they are already in it.

.ti -10
-x	Extract the named members into files in the current
directory.  The archive is not changed.

.ti -10
-t	List the archive's members.

.ti -10
-c	Create the archive, discarding whatever was there.  With -r
it means create only if absent.

.in -10
and these may be added to any of them:

.in +10
.ta 10
.ti -10
-v	Name each member as it is processed.

.in -10
.in -5
.bd
SEE ALSO
.in +5
.br
ccc (1), ld (1), wsnm (1), asz (1)


.in -5
.bd
DIAGNOSTICS
.in +5
.br
A file that is not an object in the expected format is reported and
refused.  An archive that ends in the middle of a member is reported
as truncated.


.in -5
.bd
NOTES
.in +5
.br
A driver built with Hi-Tech format support - which is not the default,
and has not been since that compiler stopped being the bootstrap -
also takes -H to write an archive in that format.
